          {"id":"c493a0a0","elType":"widget","widgetType":"text-editor","settings":{"editor":"<p>The <strong>Badri cow</strong> is an indigenous hill breed found only in the Uttarakhand Himalayas. Named after the sacred Badrinath region, these compact cows have grazed the same Himalayan alpine meadows for centuries.</p><p>What makes Badri ghee rare:</p><ul style=\"line-height:2;padding-left:20px;\"><li><strong>The breed:</strong> One of 50 officially recognised indigenous cattle breeds of India (NBAGR registered). Not a cross-breed.</li><li><strong>The diet:</strong> Natural high-altitude grazing — alpine herbs, clean Himalayan water, no feedlot, no hormones.</li><li><strong>The milk yield:</strong> Badri cows give only ~2–3 litres/day vs. 15–20 for commercial breeds. This is why Badri ghee is rare.</li><li><strong>The process:</strong> Same traditional bilona method as our A2 Ghee — hand-churned, slow-cooked, zero shortcuts.</li></ul><p>[ADD: specific location / herd / cooperative details here.]</p>"}}
        ]},
